A||N||8||Vlad||Dog-bone bushings||||4||
Z||000000||Vlad||09-07-2000||02:04 AM||||Hi guys<P>The dog-bone rubber bushings seem to be dying. The question is if I buy a stock replaysment bushig, how difficult is it to put it into the dog-bone. And removing the old one could be another <BR>pain in the a@#$%%s ??<P>I know about that nice red one from the Fierostore, but can't afford it right now.....  Student life........(((<P><BR>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Carrolles||09-07-2000||08:45 AM||||The dogbone strut is very easy to remove/re-install.  You will see a triangular plastic cover on the strut tower right next to the strut about 4 inches on each side.  Pry this cover off and you will see the nut on the bulkhead side bolt.  I believe both bolts/nuts are 15mm.  Remove both nuts and pull back on the engine to release the strain and the bolts should slide right out.  You might have to use a pry bar to get the leverage needed to pull the engine back.  A few people posted that they but the engine in gear (with ignition off of course) and rock the car back and forth to rock the engine enough to get the slack needed.<P>On my 85SE v-6 the bushings wer not difficult to remove from the strut.  On some later models I believe the bushings are actually tapered and insert fron each side of the strut.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||the need for speed||09-07-2000||12:33 PM||||I also have a dog bone that needs replacing, the rubber is pretty bad off.I would like to get some poly bushings to replace the rubber but would also like to keep my dog bone.I emailed <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com</A>  but they told me that they dont have any replacement bushings for my 87 gt.DOSE ANY ONE KNOW IF THERE IS A PLACE TO GET JUST THE URETHANE BUSHING TO REPLACE THE OLD RUBBER FOR AN 87 GT.thx ||||reg||1
