Z||000004||Vlad||09-07-2000||03:44 PM||||V6 GT 85||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-07-2000||03:47 PM||||You can't get new rubber parts for an OE bone. The only things you can do is either get another OE strut, get an aftermarket strut, or install polly in the original one.<P>You can only install polly in certain struts. The "new style" strut is not polly compatible.<P>The aftermarket struts are only a few dollars more than the OE ones.<P>read my cave article about the struts. At the bottom it explains the diferance for 4 cylinder motors. I think it's the same rule for V6. You can ask Fiero Store to make sure. (I hope to add pictures to it next week.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||09-07-2000||09:19 PM||||Kind of a trade off...no shakes and smooth operation vs. crisp shifts and no clunks...<P>So there's no way to have one poly bushing and one rubber ? Hard to believe there aren't any rubber bushings out there that will fit these bones. I've had poly in mine for a year and it hasn't softened up much. Frankly, got tired of people asking what was wrong with my car.||||reg||1
Z||000007||daved||09-07-2000||09:48 PM||||I was thinking of using the dogbone out of other GM cars...like my Lumina van.  The Fiero V6 one is 4 1/4" centres, and the van (typical for GM) is 4 3/4".  I think I could remove the 'bone mounts, and re-drill the holes further apart.  Looks to me as if there is enough room; what do you guys think?||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-07-2000||11:57 PM||||D8 You need to find an old style stock strut and only put one set of polly in it. That's the only way.||||reg||1
